<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Camino de Santiago</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Way of St James, Way of Saint James</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Camino de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>, or Way of St James, is the network of pilgrimage routes leading to the shrine of the apostle St James at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>. From the 9th and especially the 11th and 12th centuries it drew pilgrims from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the main route across northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> became lined with towns, churches and hospices. It remains a major pilgrimage and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/camino-de-santiago.jpg" alt="Camino de Santiago" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manfred Zentgraf, Volkach, Germany · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ways_of_St._James_in_Europe.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Camino de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> is a network of pilgrim routes leading to the shrine of the apostle James in the cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, where tradition holds that his remains are buried. The main route follows an earlier Roman trade road that continues to Cape Finisterre on the Atlantic coast, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> called Finisterrae, the end of the world. Pilgrims can take one of dozens of routes, and traditionally the way begins at one's own home. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camino-frances">Camino Francés</a>, the French Way, is the most popular, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-regia">Via Regia</a> forms its last portion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Way was established in the early 9th century after the discovery of the relics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-the-great">Saint James the Great</a>, and the earliest records of visits to the shrine come from the time of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>. The earliest recorded pilgrims from beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> came in the mid-11th century, and by the early 12th century the pilgrimage was highly organised.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-callixtus-ii">Pope Callixtus II</a> promoted it and began the Compostelan Holy Years. The Codex Calixtinus, published around 1140, served as the guide: four routes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puente-la-reina">Puente la Reina</a>, and from there a defined road crossed northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a>, León and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astorga">Astorga</a>.</p><p>Hospitals along the route met the pilgrims' needs, and contributed to the modern idea of the hospital. Romanesque churches with massive arches were built for the crowds, and pilgrims took home a Galician scallop shell as proof of completion, which became the badge of the pilgrim. The Church also imposed the pilgrimage as penance for grave sins. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> and political unrest in the 16th century brought a decline, though in 1779 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-adams">John Adams</a> followed part of the route in reverse on his way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. Few modern pilgrimages predate the 1957 publication of Walter Starkie's The Road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Whenever St James's Day, 25 July, falls on a Sunday, the cathedral declares a Holy Year. In 1987 the Camino was named the first Cultural Route of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-europe">Council of Europe</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camino-frances">French Way</a> and the Northern routes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> were inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List, followed by the routes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1998. Pilgrims come mostly on foot, some by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bicycle">bicycle</a> or horse, and many walk the final 100 kilometres to earn the Compostela certificate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Camino de Santiago” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The cult of Saint James at Compostela, promoted by Alfonso II and Alfonso III, grew into one of the three great pilgrimages of medieval Christendom, and the <b>Way of Saint James</b> carried people, money and ideas from France and beyond into northern Spain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-asturias">Kingdom of Asturias · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The county's towns, Saldaña and Carrión, lay on the route that became the <b>Way of St James</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-saldana">County of Saldaña</a></li></ul></section>
